# How many pages each program of this corpus draws. One basename and
# one count per line.
#
# A run compares the pages both engines drew, so a page neither of them
# drew is not in the comparison and is not missing from either side of
# it. A program that stops emitting partway through reports every page
# it reached as a match and says nothing whatever about the rest. This
# is what says how many pages there were to reach, so the run can name
# the ones it never got to.
#
# It is held from both ends: a program of this corpus with no count
# here fails the corpus, so one cannot be added without a count, and a
# count naming a program the corpus does not hold fails it too.
#
# The counts are not a record of what this tree draws -- a count taken
# from that makes whatever the tree does today correct by definition.
# These programs carry no page count of their own to read: only
# doretree has a %%Pages: line at all and it is (atend), which says
# nothing until the trailer. Each is instead a single drawing that ends
# in one showpage below the restore that closes it, which is the count
# below; ridt91 is the one whose showpage is commented out, so it draws
# none and nopage carries the reason.
#
# Counting showpage is not the same as counting pages and is not what
# was done here. alphabet has a second one inside the loop that lays
# out its text, reached only when the text outgrows the page, and its
# text does not.
alphabet 1
colorcir 1
doretree 1
escher 1
golfer 1
grayalph 1
ridt91 0
snowflak 1
spots 1
tiger 1
vasarely 1
waterfal 1
